    "modified_by": "STEP Inc",
    "description": "Grow with the great state of Ohio spanning the years 1870-1950. \n\tSpread economic growth across the state and set up an interstate rail system to transport people across the state. \n\tThis map is for more experienced players as cities and resources are spread out and industries are not always close to the cities that need them.  This map works better on computers that have more resources than the minimum requirements",
    "collection": "map",
    "thumbnail": "/img/derivatives/simple/ohio_large_v1_00/thumbnail.jpg",
    "permalink": "/map/ohio_large_v1_00",
    "lunr_id": 142
  },
  {
    "pid": "ohio_rails_v1_00",
    "label": "Ohio Rails",
    "created": "2023/08/26",
    "updated": "2023/08/26",
    "author": "slider38",
    "modified_by": "slider38",
    "description": "Welcome to Ohio Rails.  This map was designed as a one-player SAM.  It features new version 2 cars more appropriately sized for the engines pulling them.  There are no crossovers.  To cross existing track you must switch through.  The only legal way to get to Canada is via a bridge from Detroit to Windsor which is required anyway.  I hope you enjoy Ohio Rails!",
    "collection": "map",
    "thumbnail": "/img/derivatives/simple/ohio_rails_v1_00/thumbnail.jpg",
    "permalink": "/map/ohio_rails_v1_00",
    "lunr_id": 143
  },
  {
    "pid": "oldenburg_v1_00",
    "label": "Oldenburg SAM",
    "created": "2015/10/18",
    "updated": "2015/10/18",
    "author": "Lama_RT",
    "modified_by": "Lama_RT",
    "description": "A latecomer in railway construction, the Grand Duchy of Oldenburg built a network that made the most of a thinly settled, economically weak country.\n\tWhen economic development finally picked up toward the end of the nineteenth century, however, the railroad was there to allow farmers access to markets.\n\n*Special Rules*\n\tOn this map, everything is much slower than you may expect.  Industries produce at a leisurely pace, and depots take longer to put together a consist and load a train.  Upgrading stations is more expensive than usual.\n\tFor that reason, it may make sense to leave a train's consist intact over several local stops, even if the cars will remain empty part of the way.  You save valuable time by changing consists only at stations or terminals, if at all.\n\tTo boost production of swine and livestock, supply these farms with grain.  Post offices, court houses, and the government ministries in the capital city of Oldenburg will produce mail when supplied with passengers.",
    "collection": "map",
    "thumbnail": "/img/derivatives/simple/oldenburg_v1_00/thumbnail.jpg",
    "permalink": "/map/oldenburg_v1_00",
    "lunr_id": 144
  },
